ity beyond the injection of  capital they could have provided flickr  with the kind of engineering expertise  they needed to take it to the next level  in reality it was kind of a disaster  yahoo's size also created bureaucracy  office politics and a frustrating  corporate culture they stayed for the  three year earn out period but katarina  eric and jason left as soon as they  could steward actually stayed on a  little longer although by the middle of  2008 he was gone too his surreal  resignation email which mentioned his  desire to tend to his small but growing  alpaca herd and get back to tin his  first love was an instant classic  reflecting on his time with yahoo he  called the company a terrible joke and  said that innovation at flickr turned  blue and choked out after the  acquisition but moments of adversity  often contain the seeds of future  success steward hated his time at yahoo  but he learned a lot about how he didn't  want to build his next company and he  would apply those lessons in spectacular  style burnt out stewart went back to  vancouver to gather his thoughts  unfortunately he did so by himself  around this time he and katarina split  up katarina became a really successful  entrepreneur in her own right so their  story did kind of have a happy ending  cal eric and jason got in touch with  stewart and told him that they wanted to  work on something together again it's  now early 2009 the recession which would  spill over into the global financial  crisis was beginning to bite that wasn't  the only way the world had changed  though online multiplayer gaming had  grown up world of warcraft was a huge  hit so the team of four stepped back  into that world but they didn't go back  to ludicorp or game never ending they  decided to start something new and they  were determined to do things differently  they called their new company tiny spec  and the game glitch stewart and his team  specifically designed glitch to be  non-violent they were fascinated by its  potential for mutual creation and  collaboration it was idealistic in an  age before peaceful co-op games like  stardew valley and animal crossing it  actually seemed kind of quaint and again  there it is like a lighthouse in the fog  that obsession with creating better ways  for us to connect thanks to stewart's  profile and track record they were able  to quickly raise 17.5 million from high  profile investors like a16z and excel  with money in the bank tiny spec was  able to scale up they hired about 40  engineers and designers spread across  north america they encountered a problem  how could they collaborate effectively  as a team when they were scattered to  the winds it turned out this problem was  tailor-made for stewart's obsession at  first the glitch team used irc internet  relay chats you know i put on my robe  and wizard hat that kind of stuff chat  rooms basically irc did a serviceable  job of helping the tiny spec team  onboard new starters but they were  engineers they were constantly looking  for ways to optimize the team realized  that they could build in new features  around irc to make it more user friendly  they built a bunch of new tools and  called the system line feed they noticed  that it really enhanced their  productivity so much so that they were  able to launch glitch in september of  2011. it seemed to have all the key  ingredients of a massive success a great  team which had been backed by renowned  investors launching an exciting new  product to lots of fanfare and it was a  beautiful utopian idea for a game  unfortunately it was also a big flop  whether it was because people didn't  understand it or just plain didn't like  it by november glitch was back in beta a  stewart put it they basically unlaunched  and tried to tinker with it in order to  actually find an audience it took a year  of trying until october 2012 for stuart  to come to the most difficult  realization that any founder can have  things were not going to work out  although there was still about five  million dollars in the bank revenue  wasn't growing quickly enough to really  scale the business  stewart wrote an email to his board  explaining his decision then he called  an all hands meeting and broke the bad  news to his staff everyone except for  the four co-founders and four other  staff were let go stewart even had to  tell an employee who had just relocated  his family across the country that he no  longer had a job it was gut wrenching  but he was determined to do things the  right way the remnants of the team  created a website called hire a genius  and created profiles for all the former  glitch employees to help them find their  next jobs they even uploaded all the  code and all the art files from glitch  to the public domain as a demonstration  of what they built together and that  email i mentioned earlier the one stuart  wrote to his board john o'farrell was  the board member from andres and  horowitz he forwarded stewart's email to  marc andreessen mark's concise reply  showed not only their faith in stewart  but also how they grew into such a  legendary vc firm he wrote that it is  what it is if history repeats itself and  they come up with the next flicker all  will still end well mark stewart and his  team still had about four million  dollars in cash they had a great  development team they had faithful  investors and they had an idea this idea  would become the ultimate expression of  the passion that animated stuart  butterfield's life and career building  new ways for people to connect but marc  andreessen was wrong they didn't come up  with another flicker they built  something much bigger encouraged by  their experience in building line feed  the internal chat used by the glitch  team and flickr  stewart and his team decided that they  wanted to build a new internal chat  platform but they weren't the only ones  with that idea hipchat which launched in  2010 was also attempting to build an  internal work chat it took off and  before long local developers were  boasting about working at places which  used hipchat atlassian pounced an  acquired hipchat in march of 2012. the  emergence of multiple enterprise  communication platforms all around the  same time drives home something which is  really important for founders to  understand you can have a great idea you  can be a brilliant manager you can  optimize for everything under your  control but you still need things to  align for basically the last 20 years  it's been possible to boil down what  enables modern tech businesses to  succeed to just three things one the  emergence of fast stable internet and  mobile technology two the creation of  new social use cases and three a  business model that enables frictionless  scaling this is the golden triad that so  many modern tech companies wind up  satisfying and then growing massively  tiny spec realized that the moment for  line feed had arrived so they pivoted  and committed 100  to building something that could take on  and beat the competition it's easy to  assume that investors would be jumping  all over them after all steward had a  track record of building a cool company  that people loved and flickr people also  knew him and liked him but no matter how  you sliced it tiny speck had failed  investors weren't sure if stewart was up  for the grind of building a company that  could grow beyond enthusiastic early  adopters and the market was already  becoming more crowded but excel and  andreas and horowitz kept the faith and  maintained their support everyone agreed  that one of the first things the new  company needed was a catchier name  linefeed wouldn't cut it so naturally  stewart and the team came up with  searchable log of all conversation and  knowledge slack for short thankfully the  full name was basically never used in  any pitch decks or marketing materials  but it's a clue to how the team was  thinking the next step was to build an  attractive user interface if you  remember irc it was really bare bones in  terms of appearance and usability the  team worked hard to build the ideal user  interface and it worked it was beautiful  innovative and full of integrations  stewart and the team had a great early  stage product but they had a problem  they struggled to convince their friends  at other companies to use it time and  time again they'd approached them only  to be told we already used hipchat why  would we change when you're trying to  persuade people that they should  implement what is basically an entirely  new way of communicating at work you  need to nail the messaging you need to  convince them that this use case is  meaningfully different from email that  sending dozens or even hundreds of  messages back and forth every day will  actually enhance productivity and not  destroy it and that it can improve team  culture the contagion effect that we see  so often in consumer and enterprise  software only works if new users become  evangelists for the product they have to  love it enough to tell others to use it  that's what happened with slack social  media helped to build the buzz the team  launched slack in a preview release in  august of 2013. 8 000 companies signed  up for the wait list and only two weeks  later 15 000 companies had signed up  they actually had to stagger the launch  so they could add enough server capacity  for all the demand to be met by february  2014 10 000 new users were joining slack  every single day they already had 150  000 paying users do you remember how  earlier i talked about the golden triad  of modern tech companies technological  feasibility social use case and scaling  business model well it was now time for  stuart and his team to develop the  business model that would secure their  success to do so they drew on their  experience in the gaming world lots of  people would have probably set the bar  for the paid version of a product like  slack at the number of users within an  organization slack did something  different they could see that linearly  scaling the cost by the number of users  would make slack a really tough sell for  big organizations so rather than the  number of users they set the pay gate at  the number of messages in the archive  that you could access think about it if  you're working for a dynamic move fast  and break stuff type company you're  probably sending hundreds of messages a  day and you're probably not spending  much time on proper documentation so  imagine if you make a big breakthrough  on a tough project and then you're not  able to go back and retrace your steps  or imagine how hard it would be to join  a company like that if you weren't able  to get up to speed by reading important  archived messages there were other  things too like a cap on the number of  integrations you can add to the free  version compared to the paid version  plus slack's ui was always more  appealing to non-developers tons of  government departments political offices  and not-for-profits use slack but the  core of the business model was that once  a company got hooked on slack they would  become dependent on it for their project  management and their onboarding of new  employees with a beautiful product and  an elegant business model in place  stewart was ready to build a great team  he even rehired developers from his old  tiny spec crew yet again we see his  innate understanding of the value of  staying connected slack started going to  the moon its revenues grew from 12  million dollars in 2014 to 30 million  dollars the following year 100 million  in 2016 200 million in 2017 in the  summer of 2018 with revenues of 221  million and 8 million daily active users  slack cemented its dominance over  hipchat by buying it this really sealed  it slack had become unassailable in the  enterprise chat market later that year  rumors were starting to circulate that  slack was about to go public by this  stage stewart had fielded tons of  acquisition offers after the bad  experience of yahoo acquiring flickr he  knew he wanted to wait for the right  opportunity but there was a big elephant  in the room slack may have conquered  hipchat but microsoft which was still  the biggest game in town when it came to  enterprise software had launched teams  in march of 2017. it was able to  leverage microsoft's huge user base to  very quickly become the largest  enterprise communications platform  around stewart wasn't exactly pleased by  microsoft's entry into the market slack  brought an anti-trust complaint against  microsoft in the european union in  mid-2020 alleging that microsoft has  illegally tied its team's product into  its market dominant office productivity  suite and made it too hard to uninstall  it i won't discuss the details of that  ongoing case in this video but it's safe  to say that there's some bad blood  between microsoft and slack slack's  other challenge was very familiar to  anyone with a knowledge of modern tech  companies it had never actually  generated a profit it had a great  product and a solid business model but  it was totally focused on growth not  squeezing more money out of existing  users but they had a lot of cash and  capacity to generate more of it and  their product and branding meant that  they didn't need to market themselves to  prospective investors which meant that  there was less of a need for bankers and  their big fees to get involved stuart  didn't really need to raise more money  for the company this meant that a direct  public offering a dpo rather than an ipo  could be the best option it's an  approach which often makes sense for a  consumer-facing company like slack or  spotify which went public via a dpo in  2018. slack announced their intention to  file a dpo in april of 2019. on thursday  june 20th shares of slack began trading  they closed at 38.62  up almost 50 percent in a single day and  equivalent to a 19.5 billion dollar  market cap of course that's not the end  of the story the pressure of competing  with big tech companies led the share  price to fall as low as 16  things weren't looking good but after  months of speculation salesforce the  leader in crm completed its acquisition  of slack for 27.7 billion dollars in  mid-2021 stuart butterfield had taken  the natural human desire to connect and  used it to build a massive tech company  worth tens of billions of dollars but  the story doesn't end there discord  offers many of the same features as  slack and is now growing even faster so  in order to understand how discord is  changing communication you have to watch  this video about how the founder managed  to become a billionaire without going to  an ivy league school or ever working at  a big tech company it's a really  fascinating story thanks for watching
 

